---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/docs/CodeBlock.astro";
import ImagePlaceholder from "../components/docs/ImagePlaceholder.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Changelog - npkill 1.0"
  description="What's new in npkill 1.0 - profiles, panels, multiselect, filters, and more."
>
  <main class="changelog-page">
    <article class="changelog-content">
      <header class="changelog-header">
        <span class="version-badge">v1.0.0</span>
        <h1>npkill 1.0 is here</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">The rules of the game have changed</p>
        <time datetime="2026-02-01">February 1, 2026</time>
      </header>

      <div class="tldr">
        <h2>TL;DR</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>npkill works the same by default — nothing to relearn</li>
          <li>
            Now it can clean up much more than just <code>node_modules</code>
          </li>
          <li>It's faster, safer, and more flexible</li>
          <li>You don't have to learn anything new if you don't want to</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p>
        Over time, I discovered that similar solutions started appearing for
        other environments. That's when I realized the problem wasn't just in
        Node.js.
      </p>
      <p>
        Not only that, but in day-to-day work, although <code>node_modules</code
        > stole the spotlight, they weren't even close to being the biggest problem
        (even if they stood out the most).
      </p>
      <p>
        Temporary files, cache files, build artifacts... All of them add up.
      </p>

      <nav class="toc">
        <h3>Contents</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#whats-new">What's new</a></li>
          <li><a href="#whats-changed">What's changed</a></li>
          <li><a href="#bug-fixes">Bug fixes</a></li>
          <li><a href="#acknowledgements">Acknowledgements</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>

      <h2 id="whats-new">What's New</h2>

      <h3 id="profiles">Profiles — More than a node_modules tool</h3>
      <p>
        This is probably the most important change in npkill's history. And in a
        few seconds, you'll understand why.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now there isn't a default <code>target</code>, but rather a <strong
          >profile</strong
        >: <code>node</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Until now, npkill was designed to work with a single <code>target</code
        >, which by default was <code>node_modules</code>. Profiles bring
        multiple targets under one umbrella.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <code>node</code> profile includes <code>node_modules</code>, but
        also other heavy and expendable directories like <code>.next</code>, <code
          >.nx</code
        >, <code>.cache</code>, or <code>.angular</code>, among others.
      </p>
      <p>
        But that's not all. By default, npkill now includes many other profiles
        like <code>python</code>, which helps you deal with heavy <code
          >__pycache__</code
        >, <code>.venv</code>, or <code>.pytest_cache</code> directories.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Do you see the power of profiles?</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        We've included 17 profiles to help developers working with Godot, Rust,
        Unity, and more. And of course, you can create your own thanks to the <a
          href="#npkillrc">.npkillrc</a
        > file.
      </p>
      <p>
        Don't worry — this change won't affect you. By default, npkill will
        continue listing
        <code>node_modules</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can see all available profiles by running <code
          >npkill --profiles</code
        > or check out the <a href="/docs/profiles">profiles documentation</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 id="panels">Panels</h3>
      <p>
        Panels add a new dimension to npkill. Previously, the only view
        available in the TUI was the results list.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now we've added 3 new views transparently. That is, you can use them if
        you want, and if not, it won't affect how you used npkill before.
      </p>
      <p>
        These panels are shown in the top bar, and by default <code>DELETE</code
        > is displayed.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can navigate between them laterally with <kbd>←</kbd>/<kbd>h</kbd> or
        <kbd>→</kbd>/<kbd>l</kbd>.
      </p>

      <h4>Result Information</h4>
      <p>
        From the results view, you can press <kbd>→</kbd> to see details about the
        result under the cursor.
      </p>
      <p>
        Among the details, you'll find the exact date of the last file
        modification, whether it can be safely deleted, and information about
        the specific directory.
      </p>
      <p>
        The full path is also shown, so you can copy it or open the parent
        directory with <kbd>o</kbd>.
      </p>

      <ImagePlaceholder
        alt="npkill info panel"
        description="The info panel shows details about the selected directory including last modification date and risk analysis."
      />

      <h4>Live Options Panel</h4>
      <p>
        With the <strong>options panel</strong>, you can modify any parameter
        without restarting npkill and specifying them with flags.
      </p>
      <p>For example, you can change the sort order of results in real time.</p>
      <p>
        You can also change the <code>cwd</code> or targets. When you do, npkill
        will restart the search with the new settings.
      </p>

      <ImagePlaceholder
        alt="npkill options panel"
        description="The options panel allows you to change settings like sort order, targets, and working directory on the fly."
      />

      <h4>Help Panel</h4>
      <p>
        The number of options and shortcuts has kept growing. To prevent you
        from having to memorize them all and to ensure you don't miss powerful
        shortcuts, I've added the "help" panel where you can consult all the
        possibilities for your workflow.
      </p>

      <ImagePlaceholder
        alt="npkill help panel"
        description="The help panel shows all available keyboard shortcuts and commands."
      />

      <h3 id="multiselect">Multiselect — Optimizing your workflow</h3>
      <p>
        This new mode allows you to select multiple results to delete them all
        at once, instead of going one by one.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can toggle this mode with the <kbd>t</kbd> key.
      </p>
      <p>In multiselect mode, the controls change to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><kbd>Space</kbd>: Select/deselect the current result</li>
        <li><kbd>Enter</kbd>: Delete selected results</li>
        <li><kbd>a</kbd>: Select/deselect all</li>
        <li><kbd>v</kbd>: Select a range of results</li>
        <li><kbd>t</kbd>: Cancel and exit without deleting</li>
      </ul>

      <ImagePlaceholder
        alt="npkill multiselect demo"
        description="Multiselect mode in action - select multiple directories and delete them all at once."
        aspectRatio="16/9"
      />

      <h3 id="filter">Result Filter — We didn't know we needed it</h3>
      <p>
        Now that npkill has become a much more useful tool, we need ways to
        comfortably manage all the findings it discovers.
      </p>
      <p>This new feature lets you filter visible results.</p>
      <p>Is this useful? Very.</p>
      <p>
        Because simply by pressing <kbd>/</kbd>, you can not only check if a
        certain directory is present and see its info, but also <strong
          >filter by paths</strong
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        Imagine you do a full scan of your home and npkill finds tens of
        gigabytes to free up in hundreds of different places scattered
        everywhere. Instead of running npkill in each sub-directory, you can
        directly type that path and check the results one by one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Of course, you can also combine it with <strong>multiselect</strong> mode
        to delete everything matching your filter in one pass.
      </p>
      <p>
        And if you're really pro, <strong>you can use regex</strong>. What more
        could you ask for?
      </p>

      <ImagePlaceholder
        alt="npkill search filter"
        description="Filter results by path or regex pattern."
      />

      <h3 id="npkillrc">.npkillrc</h3>
      <p>
        You probably belong to the group of people who use this tool regularly
        as a maintenance and cleanup routine. And you're probably tired of
        launching it while always defining your personal parameters (unless you
        have an alias in your shell).
      </p>
      <p>
        In this optional file, you can define npkill's default behavior. From
        the
        <code>cwd</code> or default <strong>profile</strong>, to settings or
        custom profiles — you can define it all here.
      </p>
      <p>
        By default, npkill looks for the file <code>~/.npkillrc</code> at startup,
        but you can also specify it with the <code>--config</code> or <code
          >-c</code
        > flag.
      </p>
      <p>The priority order for options is:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Options defined in <code>.npkillrc</code></li>
        <li>Options defined in CLI during launch</li>
        <li>Options modified (temporarily) in the options panel</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Check out the <a href="/docs/npkillrc"
          >complete .npkillrc documentation</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h4>Custom Profiles</h4>
      <p>
        You can create profiles to your liking by defining them in the <code
          >.npkillrc</code
        > file.
      </p>

      <CodeBlock
        code={`{
  "profiles": {
    "webdev": {
      "description": "Frontend web development artifacts and build outputs",
      "targets": ["dist", ".next", ".nuxt", ".output"]
    },
    "mystack": {
      "description": "Full-stack project artifacts (JS/Python/Java)",
      "targets": ["venv", ".venv", "target", "__pycache__", ".gradle"]
    },
    "mobile": {
      "description": "Mobile platform build folders and caches",
      "targets": ["Pods", "build", "DerivedData", "gradle"]
    }
  }
}`}
        lang="json"
        filename="~/.npkillrc"
      />

      <p>
        The profiles you define will override the pre-defined ones in npkill.
      </p>

      <h3>Result Counter</h3>
      <p>
        In the upper right corner, you'll see a summary of the results that have
        been found, as well as the active <strong>profiles</strong>.
      </p>

      <ImagePlaceholder
        alt="Result counter"
        description="The result counter shows found items and active profiles."
        aspectRatio="4/1"
      />

      <h3>Scrollbar</h3>
      <p>
        How much is left until the end? Now you can get an idea with the <code
          >scrollbar</code
        >, which gives you a sense of your current position relative to the
        results list.
      </p>

      <ImagePlaceholder
        alt="Scrollbar demo"
        description="The new scrollbar shows your position in the results list."
        aspectRatio="4/3"
      />

      <h3 id="json-mode">JSON Mode</h3>
      <p>
        Two new flags have been added that completely disable the TUI and only
        print results:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <code>--json</code>: Collects all results and prints them as a single
          JSON object.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>--json-stream</code>: Prints each result as an independent JSON
          object on its own line as soon as it's found. Useful for real-time
          processing or when dealing with large scans where you want to start
          processing results immediately.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>API</h3>
      <p>
        Before, the entire package was one unit. That is, npkill was only what
        you saw — what you could interact with directly.
      </p>
      <p>
        I've refactored the project to separate the <strong>core engine</strong>
        (<code>core</code>) from everything related to the <strong>TUI</strong> (<code
          >cli</code
        >).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What does this mean for the average user?</strong> Nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What does this mean for the advanced user?</strong> That now you
        can import the <code>core</code> of <code>npkill</code> in any Node.js program
        and use it programmatically. Whether to create your own implementations using
        the project's powerful search engine.
      </p>

      <h2 id="whats-changed">What's Changed</h2>

      <h3>Risk Analyzer</h3>
      <p>
        Not all found directories are bad. For example, some applications like
        Spotify or Discord need their own <code>node_modules</code> intact to work
        correctly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Path patterns have been improved to make these warnings more reliable.
        Additionally, you can see an explanation of why it was marked as
        "sensitive" in the <strong>info panel</strong>.
      </p>

      <h3>Fluidity and Optimization</h3>
      <p>
        npkill handles search operations at a very low level. I'm always
        tweaking parameters to achieve the best performance and speed values.
      </p>
      <p>Size calculation has been decoupled from the main thread.</p>

      <h3>Automatic Unit Adjustment</h3>
      <p>
        Before, everything was shown in <code>MB</code>. Now, unless you specify
        it with
        <code>--size-unit</code>, the unit will adapt between <code>MB</code> and
        <code>GB</code>.
      </p>

      <h3>More Stability</h3>
      <p>General stability improvements across the board.</p>

      <h3>Removed Color Customization</h3>
      <p>
        Until now, it was possible to adjust the cursor color with <code
          >--color</code
        >, but I've removed it because it didn't add much beyond complexity.
      </p>
      <p>
        However, there's a better explanation: you can now adjust that color and
        all others by adjusting the <em>colorscheme</em> of your terminal. So it's
        something you surely won't miss.
      </p>

      <h3>Other Changes</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Changed <code>--target</code> to <code>--targets</code> to specify a list
          of directories
        </li>
        <li>Changed the text <code>Last_mod</code> to <code>Age</code></li>
        <li>Improved "No results" message</li>
        <li>
          Style improvements when <em>size</em> or <em>age</em> is being calculated
        </li>
        <li>Legibility improvements</li>
        <li>Improved <code>--help</code> output</li>
        <li>Simplified banner</li>
        <li>Symbolic links are now ignored during search</li>
        <li>
          Fixed result flickering when a sort criterion is defined and both have
          the same value
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="bug-fixes">Bug Fixes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>TTY</strong>: The TTY restriction for launching npkill has
          been removed.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="acknowledgements">Acknowledgements</h2>
      <p>
        This version of npkill 1.0 is the result of years of feedback, bug
        reports, and contributions. None of this would be what it is today
        without all of you and the love you've given the project all this time.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you like npkill, don't hesitate to leave us a big ⭐️ on
        <a href="https://github.com/voidcosmos/npkill">GitHub</a> and
        <a
          href="https://github.com/voidcosmos/npkill?tab=readme-ov-file#coffee-buy-us-a-coffee"
          >support us financially</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>And of course, don't hesitate to try all these changes with:</p>

      <CodeBlock code="npx npkill@latest" />

      <p>
        See you very soon!<br />
        <em>— Zaldih</em>
      </p>
    </article>

    <Footer />
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .changelog-page {
    background: var(--color-bg-dark);
    min-height: 100vh;
  }

  .changelog-content {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .changelog-header {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .version-badge {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--color-primary);
    color: white;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    padding: 0.35rem 1rem;
    border-radius: 100px;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .changelog-header h1 {
    font-size: 2.5rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .subtitle {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }

  time {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--color-text-subtle);
  }

  .tldr {
    background: var(--color-bg-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }

  .tldr h2 {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--color-primary);
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .tldr ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  .tldr li {
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }

  .toc {
    background: var(--color-bg-darker);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }

  .toc h3 {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .toc ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .toc a {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    margin: 3rem 0 1.5rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  h3 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 1rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  h4 {
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0.75rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  p {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  ul,
  ol {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .changelog-content {
      padding: 2rem 1rem;
    }

    .changelog-header h1 {
      font-size: 2rem;
    }

    h2 {
      font-size: 1.5rem;
    }

    .toc ul {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 0.5rem;
    }
  }
</style>
